    "question": {
      "difficulty": "EASY",
      "content": "Write a Python function that computes the **gradient of the Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss with respect to the output of a single neuron** during backpropagation. This is the elementary backward-pass operation that feeds the chain rule for updating weights in a neural network.\n\nThe MSE loss for a single sample is:\n\n```\nL = (y_pred - y_true)^2\n```\n\nThe gradient of L with respect to y_pred is:\n\n```\ndL/dy_pred = 2 * (y_pred - y_true)\n```\n\nGiven y_pred and y_true, return dL/dy_pred for each sample.\n\n**Parameters**\n\n- `y_true` (list[float]): True target values\n- `y_pred` (list[float]): Predicted output values from the neuron\n\n**Requirements**\n\n- Compute the element-wise gradient using the formula above\n- Return the result as a list of floats rounded to four decimal places\n- Do not use any autograd or automatic differentiation library\n\n**Returns**\n\n- list[float]: The gradient of the MSE loss with respect to each predicted value, rounded to four decimal places",
      "short_text": "Backprop: MSE Loss Gradient",
